hume-dialogues-3.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps hume-dialogues-3.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "Cleanthes' principles hold" is true and "nothing is gained by the hypothesis that the deities arose from the unknown powers of nature, since it is as easy to suppose men and animals sprung immediately from a like origin" and "a numerous society of deities is as explicable as one universal deity possessing the powers and perfections of the whole society" are false. Is S2, "Push the same inference a step further, and you will find a numerous society of deities as explicable as one universal deity, who possesses within himself the powers and perfections of the whole society.", true then?
